SECTIONS = {

103: (None, [
 ("In That — What the Sign Refers To", [
  "Truly in that is a sign. The commentators identify the referent: in that is a sign refers to the previous stories of the destroyed nations (JJ). The sign is not a single event or a particular miracle but the whole sequence the sūrah has just narrated.",
  "This is the verse where the sūrah states what the narratives were for. Everything from Noah through Shuʿayb, with the account of Moses inserted before the close, has been building toward this claim — that the pattern of warning, refusal, and destruction is itself evidence. The commentators' identification of the referent settles a question the verse leaves open: it does not say in what the sign consists, and without the gloss a reader might take it to mean the Day itself rather than the stories that precede it."]),
 ("For Those Who Fear", [
  "The sign is specified as a sign for those who fear the punishment of the Hereafter. The qualification restricts who the sign functions as a sign for, and the restriction is not arbitrary.",
  "A pattern of warning followed by destruction is evidence only to someone who takes the possibility of punishment seriously. To a reader who does not fear the Hereafter, the same narratives are history — accounts of towns that were destroyed, with no bearing on anything to come. The verse says the sign is there, and says who it is a sign for, and the two statements together mean that the evidence is not coercive. It is available, and its availability depends on a disposition the verse names rather than produces."]),
 ("A Day on Which Mankind Shall Be Gathered", [
  "That is a Day on which mankind shall be gathered together. The commentators explain that mankind will all be gathered on the Day of Judgment in order to be judged and recompensed for their actions (Bḍ), and note that the event is referred to elsewhere as the Day of Gathering, at 42:7 and 64:9.",
  "The gathering is the precondition of the judgement. Recompense for actions requires that the actors be present, and the verse states the presence before it states the judgement. The two names for the same Day — the Day of Gathering and the Day of Judgment — describe the same event from its two sides: the assembling and the reckoning that the assembling makes possible.",
  "The commentators' cross-reference at 64:9 fills out the scene: the Day of Gathering refers to the Day of Resurrection, when all human beings of all generations and all jinn will be gathered on a single open, barren plain for judgement, each community behind its prophet, as all living things are gathered unto their Lord in the end. The phrase each community behind its prophet returns to 17:71, the same verse the commentators cited for Pharaoh leading his people at v98. On that Day every community is behind someone, and the sūrah has spent its length showing what follows from who that someone is."]),
 ("A Day That Shall Be Witnessed", [
  "And that is a Day that shall be witnessed. The commentators explain the phrase: the Day of Judgment is called a Day that shall be witnessed because it will be the day on which all human beings, from the beginning of creation to its end, will be present (Kl) and stand before God (Z) when He calls them to account for their actions during their time on earth.",
  "The witnessing is therefore not the observation of bystanders but the presence of participants. Everyone who ever lived is there, and each is called to account for their own time on earth. The commentators' two glosses add different things: Kl's establishes the scope — from the beginning of creation to its end — and Z's establishes the posture, standing before God. Together they describe a gathering that is total in its extent and personal in its address."]),
 ("What the Verse Does Not Say", [
  "The verse does not say when the Day is. It states that it will be witnessed and that mankind will be gathered, and leaves the timing to the next verse. It does not describe the gathering, and does not say how it will occur or in what order.",
  "The verse also does not say that those who fear will be exempt from the gathering. The fear is presented as the disposition that makes the sign legible, not as a protection from the Day. Everyone is gathered; the difference the verse marks is in who recognises what the stories mean, and that recognition is what the fear produces."]),
]),

104: (None, [
 ("The Delay", [
  "And We delay it not, save for a limited term. The verse takes up the Day named in the previous verse and states that its postponement is real but bounded. The delay is not a cancellation, and the verse does not offer it as one.",
  "The construction is worth noting. The negation comes first — We delay it not — and the exception follows. Read literally, the verse denies that God delays the Day and then immediately qualifies the denial. What is being denied is not that there is a delay but that the delay is indefinite. The Day is coming; what separates the present from it is a term with a limit."]),
 ("A Limited Term", [
  "The commentators explain the phrase directly: a limited term refers to the fact that the time of the Day of Judgment is already known to God (R). The limitation is therefore not a matter of uncertainty but of knowledge — the term is limited because it has been fixed, and it has been fixed because it is known.",
  "This reading reverses what the phrase might otherwise suggest. A limited term could be read as a period whose end is not yet determined but will be reached. On the commentators' reading it is the opposite: the end is determined, and what is limited is the interval between now and then. The verse's assurance is not that the Day will eventually arrive but that its arrival is already settled."]),
 ("What the Delay Does Not Mean", [
  "The verse does not present the delay as mercy toward the disbelievers, and does not present it as indifference. It states the delay as a fact about the Day's timing and attaches the limit. The sūrah has been consistent that the warnings given to previous communities were genuine and that the reprieves were genuine — the commentators note at v36 that Noah had been calling his people to God for some 950 years (Aj; see 29:14: And he remained among them a thousand years, less fifty), and Shuʿayb's people were given time to reform — and the delay named here is of the same kind.",
  "The commentators do not elaborate on the purpose of the delay, and the verse does not supply one. What it establishes is that the interval is finite. The reader who takes the previous narratives as a sign will read the delay accordingly: as the same kind of interval that previous communities were given, and one that closed."]),
 ("The Verse in the Sequence", [
  "The two verses form a pair with the narratives that precede them. Verse 103 says the stories are a sign for those who fear the punishment of the Hereafter. Verse 104 says the Day is delayed for a limited term. Together they close the narrative section by turning it toward the reader's own situation.",
  "The movement is from what happened to what is pending. The destroyed nations are past; the Day is future; the interval between is the reader's time. The verse does not say how long the interval is, and the commentators note only that its length is known to God. The reader is left with the fact of the limit rather than its measure, which is precisely the position the previous communities were in."]),
 ("What the Verse Does Not Say", [
  "The verse does not say how long the term is. It does not give a measure, and does not say whether it is long or short in human terms. It does not say whether the term is the same for all people or differs.",
  "The verse also does not say that the delay can be extended. It states the limit without qualification, and does not offer the possibility that reform might lengthen the interval — though the sūrah's treatment of previous communities at v117 suggests that the condition of a people matters to whether they are destroyed in this world. The verse concerns the Day, and the Day's term is stated as fixed."]),
]),

117: (None, [
 ("The Negative Claim", [
  "And thy Lord would never destroy the towns unjustly. The verse states a limit on divine action in the strongest available terms. The construction denies not merely that God does destroy towns unjustly but that He would — the possibility is excluded rather than the occurrence.",
  "This is the verse that the commentators at vv 100–102 pointed forward to when they noted that God destroyed the towns while they were doing wrong, although if they had changed their ways He might not have destroyed them. The connection is explicit in their note, and it is what keeps the five destructions narrated earlier in the sūrah from being read as arbitrary. Each occurred under a condition, and this verse states the condition's converse.",
  "The verse also answers the question posed at v116: So why were there not among the generations before you those possessing merit, who would forbid corruption upon the earth, other than a few of those whom We saved among them? The Shiite commentator al-Ṭūsī observes that in asking that question God intends to produce wonder and amazement in the Prophet — that in past communities there were only a few who were righteous while the rest were disbelievers who deserved punishment. Verse 117 then supplies the limit: the few who forbade corruption were enough to keep a town from unjust destruction, and where there were none, destruction came."]),
 ("The Condition", [
  "While their people were reforming. The clause is the verse's real content. The denial of unjust destruction is qualified by a state of affairs in the towns themselves, and the qualification is what makes the verse a rule rather than an assertion.",
  "Read together with the first clause, the verse says that destruction does not come to a people who are reforming. The commentators at vv 100–102 make the same point from the other direction: the punishments were not arbitrary but followed from iniquities the people had committed. The two statements are the same rule stated once as a negative and once as a positive, and the sūrah gives both."]),
 ("The Reading About Fair Dealings", [
  "The commentators record a more specific interpretation. Some take v117 to mean that God would not destroy a people merely because they were disbelievers if their worldly transactions and conduct could be characterised as fair and honest. On this reading the condition in the verse is not belief but conduct, and the reform mentioned is reform in dealings rather than in creed.",
  "This is a consequential reading and the commentators preserve it without endorsing it. If it is right, then a community's destruction turns on how its members treat one another rather than on what they believe, and disbelief alone does not bring destruction. The commentators do not resolve the question, and the verse's wording — while their people were reforming — is general enough to admit either reading."]),
 ("The Four Peoples Named", [
  "The commentators support the specific reading by appeal to the narratives. The peoples of Noah, Ṣāliḥ, Lot, and Shuʿayb were destroyed utterly when God characterised them as harming not only their own souls but also other human beings and other creatures (Q, R).",
  "The list is instructive. Hūd's people — ʿĀd — are not in it, and the commentators do not say why. The four named are the four whose wrongdoing the sūrah described in social terms: Noah's people mocked and rejected, Ṣāliḥ's hamstrung the she-camel, Lot's assaulted their guests, Shuʿayb's cheated in the measure and the balance. In each case the harm extended beyond the harmers. The commentators' argument is that this is what distinguished the destroyed from the merely disbelieving."]),
 ("What the Verse Does Not Say", [
  "The verse does not define reforming. It does not say whether reform must be collective or individual, gradual or immediate, or whether it must be motivated by faith. The commentators' two readings differ precisely on this, and the verse does not settle it.",
  "The verse also does not say that a reforming people will never be destroyed. It says God would not destroy the towns unjustly while they were reforming, which excludes unjust destruction rather than destruction as such. Whether a reforming people could be destroyed justly, and on what grounds, the verse does not address. The commentators' note at vv 100–102 — that He might not have destroyed them had they changed their ways — is phrased with the same tentativeness."]),
]),

119: (None, [
 ("The Exception", [
  "Save those upon whom thy Lord has Mercy. The verse excepts a group from the difference described in the preceding verse, where mankind is said not to have been made one community and to cease not to differ. The exception is stated without a number and without a name.",
  "The commentators explain the background: mankind is not one community because the existence of diversity and difference — *ikhtilāf* — is decreed by the Divine Wisdom (Aj), and they cite 5:48, 16:93, 42:18, and 2:213 as parallels. As a consequence of what God's Wisdom demands, people did not cease to differ, which suggests that strife and difference are a necessary part of life in this world. Some receive the Divine Mercy and follow the truth, while others receive God's Vengeance and pursue falsehood (Aj). The exception in this verse names the first group."]),
 ("For This He Created Them", [
  "And for this He created them. The clause attaches the exception to creation itself. The commentators read it as indicating that the exempted group is not an accident within the difference but part of what creation was for.",
  "The commentators also note that they cease not to differ may refer more specifically to the religious and sectarian differences among people (Q, R). On that reading the difference the verse describes is not merely disagreement about worldly matters but division within religion, and the exception marks those who are not caught up in it. The verse does not specify, and both the general and the specific reading are preserved in the apparatus."]),
 ("Ibn ʿAjībah on Those Exempted", [
  "Ibn ʿAjībah identifies the exempted group in terms that the commentators record: they are a people whom God has guided out of His Bounty. They adhere to what is the root of religion and its foundation, such as belief in God's Oneness, and faith in all of the messengers and that with which they have come.",
  "The description is notable for what it lists and what it omits. What is listed is foundational — God's Oneness and faith in all the messengers and their messages. What is omitted is the sectarian detail that divides people within religion. On Ibn ʿAjībah's account the exempted are those who hold to the foundation and are therefore not bound up in the strife and difference the verse describes. The commentators present this as his reading rather than as the verse's explicit claim.",
  "The reading fits the verse's structure. The exception is introduced by mercy — save those upon whom thy Lord has Mercy — and Ibn ʿAjībah describes the exempted as a people whom God has guided out of His Bounty. Both formulations make the exemption something granted rather than achieved, which is consistent with the sūrah's treatment of salvation elsewhere: at vv 58, 66, and 94 the believers were saved through a mercy, and the ground named was mercy rather than merit. The same ground is named here."]),
 ("The Fulfilled Word", [
  "And the Word of thy Lord is fulfilled: I shall surely fill Hell with jinn and men all together. The verse closes with a declaration that the commentators do not gloss beyond the quotation itself. The word is described as fulfilled, in the perfect tense, for something that has not yet occurred.",
  "The tense is significant and the verse does not explain it. A word already fulfilled describes a settled matter, and the sūrah uses the same idiom elsewhere for what is certain. The juxtaposition with the exception is deliberate: some are exempted by mercy, and the rest are covered by a word already fulfilled. The verse states both without reconciling them, and the commentators record the readings of the preceding clauses without addressing the tension between them."]),
 ("What the Verse Does Not Say", [
  "The verse does not say how many are exempted, and does not say by what criterion beyond the mercy named. It does not say whether the exemption is known in this world or only in the next.",
  "The verse also does not say that the difference will end. It states that people cease not to differ and excepts a group from the difference, and the exception is not presented as a resolution. The commentators' note that strife and difference are a necessary part of life in this world is what makes the exception an exemption rather than an ending — the difference continues, and some are not bound up in it."]),
]),

}
